            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Averages angles - headings, joint angles, phases, times of day - the way they need to be averaged: around the circle.\n\nThe ordinary mean treats -170 and 170 as far apart and gives 0, pointing exactly the wrong way; they are 20 degrees apart, either side of 180, and their average is 180. Whenever angles can cross the \u00b1180 line, np.mean gives answers that are not just a little off but turned right round. scipy.circmean adds the angles as directions - arrows of length 1 - and gives the direction of the sum.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.circmean <axis: int> <units>\nDefaults: axis None (every value together), degrees.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nangles:\nThe angles (a list or array). Every input is measured and sent. nan values are skipped.\n\naxis (text box, also an inlet):\nNone measures every angle together. A number measures along that axis: for a 2-D array, 0 gives one answer per column.\n\nunits:\nAn option, under the node: degrees (the default) or radians.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmean:\nThe average angle, from -180 to 180 (or -pi to pi).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFour headings just either side of due south: 170 -170 175 -175. scipy.circmean gives 180 (shown as -180 - the same direction); np.mean gives 0, due north. A metro re-sends the angles twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.circstd measures how spread the angles are. np.unwrap removes the jumps from a run of angles. scipy.rotation_mean averages full 3-D orientations.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Averages angles - headings, joint angles, phases, times of day - the way they need to be averaged: around the circle.\n\nThe ordinary mean treats -170 and 170 as far apart and gives 0, pointing exactly the wrong way; they are 20 degrees apart, either side of 180, and their average is 180. Whenever angles can cross the \u00b1180 line, np.mean gives answers that are not just a little off but turned right round. scipy.circmean adds the angles as directions - arrows of length 1 - and gives the direction of the sum.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.circmean <axis: int> <units>\nDefaults: axis None (every value together), degrees.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nangles:\nThe angles (a list or array). Every input is measured and sent. nan values are skipped.\n\naxis (text box, also an inlet):\nNone measures every angle together. A number measures along that axis: for a 2-D array, 0 gives one answer per column.\n\nunits:\nAn option, under the node: degrees (the default) or radians.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmean:\nThe average angle, from -180 to 180 (or -pi to pi).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFour headings just either side of due south: 170 -170 175 -175. scipy.circmean gives 180 (shown as -180 - the same direction); np.mean gives 0, due north. A metro re-sends the angles twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.circstd measures how spread the angles are. np.unwrap removes the jumps from a run of angles. scipy.rotation_mean averages full 3-D orientations."
        },
